#uuid "391564c7-a751-5991-b788-dcbb08b27c2c" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553757 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 y$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 x y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=4 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=4 x.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{2}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(8 x^{3}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{4}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553764 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186553754 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553764 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553764 #edb/ref 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 28 x y, \\; 12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553764 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9f300428-dd28-5d22-81b0-c450650df73d" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553764 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=28 x y$ and $Q(x,y)=12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=28 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=12 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{3}}\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[12 x^{2} y + 14 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{3}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(x^{5} \\left(14 x + 12\\right)\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{7} + 2 x^{6}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553768 #edb/ref 1168 "generate/scratch/17592186172099.py" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186553768 #edb/ref 1169 #uuid 